2025-05-01 03:05:11 - Thread-1183 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns & their possible usage

• product_category_name_translation.product_category_name_english : to find the English string 'toys’ that identifies the category we want to analyse.  
• product_category_name_translation.product_category_name        : Portuguese alias of that category, used to link with the products table.  
• products.product_category_name                                  : join field that tells us whether a product is a toy.  
• products.product_id                                             : passed to order_items so we know which order lines are toys.  
• order_items.product_id                                          : join field with products; lets us isolate toy order‑lines.  
• order_items.order_id                                            : join field with orders.  
• order_items.price                                               : value of each toy item (optional – use if revenue is needed).  
• orders.order_id                                                 : join field; gives us purchase timestamp & status.  
• orders.order_purchase_timestamp                                 : purchase date; lets us build a daily time series (Jan‑01‑2017 → Aug‑29‑2018 for modelling and Dec‑05‑08‑2018 for prediction).  
• orders.order_status                                             : keep only 'delivered' so we count completed sales.  

Typical conditions  
  – tr.product_category_name_english = 'toys'                        (or LIKE '%toy%')  
  – orders.order_status          = 'delivered'  
  – DATE(orders.order_purchase_timestamp) BETWEEN '2017‑01‑01' AND '2018‑08‑29' (modelling period)  
  – DATE(orders.order_purchase_timestamp) BETWEEN '2018‑12‑01' AND '2018‑12‑10' (window for 5‑day MA around 5‑8 Dec‑2018)  


Ten exploratory SQL queries (all return ≤ 20 rows and use DISTINCT)

```sql
--Description: List every English category name available.
SELECT DISTINCT "product_category_name_english"
FROM "product_category_name_translation"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Show the Portuguese ↔ English mapping rows that mention “toy”.
SELECT DISTINCT "product_category_name",
       "product_category_name_english"
FROM "product_category_name_translation"
WHERE "product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Which Portuguese category names in the products table correspond to English “toy”?
SELECT DISTINCT pr."product_category_name"
FROM "products"               AS pr
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Sample of product‑IDs that belong to the toy category.
SELECT DISTINCT pr."product_id"
FROM "products"               AS pr
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Orders that contain at least one toy item.
SELECT DISTINCT oi."order_id"
FROM "order_items"            AS oi
JOIN "products"               AS pr ON oi."product_id" = pr."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Purchase date & status for a sample of toy orders.
SELECT DISTINCT o."order_id",
       o."order_purchase_timestamp",
       o."order_status"
FROM "orders"                 AS o
JOIN "order_items"            AS oi ON o."order_id" = oi."order_id"
JOIN "products"               AS pr ON oi."product_id" = pr."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Daily count of delivered toy items during the modelling period.
SELECT DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")      AS "order_date",
       COUNT(DISTINCT oi."order_item_id")      AS "toy_items_sold"
FROM "orders"                 AS o
JOIN "order_items"            AS oi ON o."order_id" = oi."order_id"
JOIN "products"               AS pr ON oi."product_id" = pr."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
  AND o."order_status" = 'delivered'
  AND DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
GROUP BY DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Daily toy revenue (sum of price) for the same period.
SELECT DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")      AS "order_date",
       SUM(oi."price")                         AS "toy_revenue_brl"
FROM "orders"                 AS o
JOIN "order_items"            AS oi ON o."order_id" = oi."order_id"
JOIN "products"               AS pr ON oi."product_id" = pr."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
  AND o."order_status" = 'delivered'
  AND DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
GROUP BY DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Training data with a numeric ‘day_index’ suitable for linear regression.
SELECT DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") AS "order_date",
       julianday(DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")) - julianday('2017-01-01') AS "day_idx",
       COUNT(oi."order_item_id")           AS "toy_items_sold"
FROM "orders"                 AS o
JOIN "order_items"            AS oi ON o."order_id" = oi."order_id"
JOIN "products"               AS pr ON oi."product_id" = pr."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
  AND o."order_status" = 'delivered'
  AND DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2017-01-01' AND '2018-08-29'
GROUP BY DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Delivered toy sales around 1–10 Dec 2018 (needed for 5‑day symmetric MA).
SELECT DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")      AS "order_date",
       COUNT(oi."order_item_id")               AS "toy_items_sold"
FROM "orders"                 AS o
JOIN "order_items"            AS oi ON o."order_id" = oi."order_id"
JOIN "products"               AS pr ON oi."product_id" = pr."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS tr
  ON pr."product_category_name" = tr."product_category_name"
WHERE tr."product_category_name_english" LIKE '%toy%'
  AND o."order_status" = 'delivered'
  AND DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp") BETWEEN '2018-12-01' AND '2018-12-10'
GROUP BY DATE(o."order_purchase_timestamp")
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries give all the pieces you need to:

1. Extract daily toy‑sales counts for 1 Jan 2017 → 29 Aug 2018,  
2. Fit a simple linear regression (day_idx → toy_items_sold),  
3. Predict daily sales for 5, 6, 7, 8 Dec 2018,  
4. Compute each 5‑day symmetric moving average (using the 2 preceding & 2 following predicted days), and  
5. Add those four averages to obtain the requested final sum.
